/* crosswalk cards, r38. */
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38a"]=[{"q": "A definition by recursion on formulas gives each formula one value. Which technique licenses that?", "a": "one-reading", "w": ["free-places", "matching-worlds", "proof-check"], "why": "Every formula is built in exactly one way, so the recursion never meets a formula twice over."}, {"q": "A statement has no free placeholder. Why is it true or false in a world outright, whatever the assignment?", "a": "the free-places technique", "w": ["the one-reading technique", "the matching-worlds technique", "the true-everywhere technique"], "why": "Truth depends only on what the assignment gives the free placeholders, and there are none."}, {"q": "h matches the members of M with those of M′ and carries every mark. A line reads “φ true in M, so φ true in M′”. Which technique?", "a": "matching-worlds", "w": ["one-reading", "free-places", "move-keeps"], "why": "A matching carries every true statement to a true one."}, {"q": "What is ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) in the world with one member?", "a": "true", "w": ["false", "neither", "not a statement"], "why": "The only member is the value of 0."}, {"q": "What does G ⊨ φ say?", "a": "φ is true in every world of G", "w": ["G reaches φ", "φ is in G", "φ is true in N"], "why": "It is a claim about worlds, not about proofs."}, {"q": "In the proof of the matching-worlds technique, where is it needed that h is onto?", "a": "at the every and the some", "w": ["at the equations", "at ¬", "at the joining words"], "why": "Every member of M′ named by ∀ must be h of some member of M."}, {"q": "Is S0 a formula?", "a": "no: it is a term", "w": ["yes: an equation", "yes: a statement", "no: it is not a string"], "why": "Terms name members; formulas are built from equations and ⊥."}, {"q": "Which of these worlds has a member that is the value of no term without placeholders?", "a": "the numbers with a second copy beside them, next acting on each copy", "w": ["the numbers", "the world with one member", "the numbers up to 10, next looping"], "why": "Terms without placeholders take values only in the first copy."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38b"]=[{"q": "A proof cites the chain P ⇒ Q, Q ⇒ R, so P ⇒ R as one line. Which technique says that is allowed?", "a": "derived-move", "w": ["proof-check", "proof-listing", "true-everywhere"], "why": "A move reached once may be used as one line and unpacked later."}, {"q": "Whether a finite list of lines is a proof from a finite G is settled by a recipe that always stops. Which technique?", "a": "proof-check", "w": ["proof-listing", "derived-move", "search-reached"], "why": "Finitely many lines, each checked against finitely many givens and moves."}, {"q": "G can be listed. Which technique lets the statements G reaches be listed too?", "a": "proof-listing", "w": ["proof-check", "derived-move", "countable-world"], "why": "List the proofs one after another and read off their last lines."}, {"q": "Speak of everything takes (S, φ(y)) to (S, ∀x φ(x)). What must hold of y?", "a": "it is free in no formula of S and no given used above", "w": ["it is free in φ", "it is the placeholder x", "nothing"], "why": "The argument used nothing about y."}, {"q": "Use an every puts x₁ in for x₀ in ∃x₁ ¬(x₀ = x₁). Is it allowed?", "a": "no: x₁ would be caught", "w": ["yes: any term may go in", "yes: x₁ is a placeholder", "no: x₀ is bound"], "why": "A term put in must not fall under an every or a some that binds its placeholder."}, {"q": "Not-not out is struck from the list. Which of these can the shorter list still reach?", "a": "¬¬(P ∨ ¬P)", "w": ["P ∨ ¬P", "¬¬P ⇒ P", "both of these"], "why": "Refute twice reaches the doubled denial; the world of stages blocks the others."}, {"q": "A 20-line proof cites 5 derived moves of 7 lines each. At most how many lines does the rewrite need?", "a": "50", "w": ["35", "140", "27"], "why": "20 − 5 + 5 · 7 = 50."}, {"q": "Is carry an if through a statement?", "a": "no: it is a move, a technique for writing lines", "w": ["yes: (P ∧ (P ⇒ Q)) ⇒ Q", "yes: an axiom", "no: it is a world"], "why": "Writing it as a line needs the move again to use it (the tortoise’s regress)."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38c"]=[{"q": "A line (S, R) holds in M when…", "a": "every assignment making all of S true makes R true", "w": ["R is true under some assignment", "S is true", "R is in S"], "why": "4a15’s check, with truth in a world in place of a row."}, {"q": "Each move, fed lines that hold in a world, writes a line that holds there. Which technique?", "a": "move-keeps", "w": ["true-everywhere", "world-means-clash-free", "derived-move"], "why": "Checked move by move."}, {"q": "G ⊢ φ. A line reads “so φ is true in every world of G”. Which technique?", "a": "true-everywhere", "w": ["move-keeps", "reached-if-true-everywhere", "two-questions"], "why": "Induction along the proof, with move-keeps at each step."}, {"q": "G has a world. A line reads “so G is clash-free”. Which technique?", "a": "world-means-clash-free", "w": ["true-everywhere", "term-world", "finite-parts"], "why": "⊥ is true in no world."}, {"q": "How does one show that G does not reach φ?", "a": "find a world of G in which φ is false", "w": ["try many proofs", "show φ is false in N", "list the proofs from G"], "why": "The true-everywhere technique, turned round."}, {"q": "Which world shows ∀x ∃y R(x, y) does not give ∃y ∀x R(x, y)?", "a": "two members, each standing in R only to itself", "w": ["one member, R true", "the numbers with <", "the empty world"], "why": "Every x has a y, but no one y serves both."}, {"q": "If worlds with no members were allowed, which move would stop keeping truth?", "a": "show an example", "w": ["self-sameness", "join", "refute"], "why": "x₀ = x₀ is an empty truth there, ∃x₀ (x₀ = x₀) does not."}, {"q": "The two adding lines, next one-to-one, 0 nobody’s next, no induction. Does that collection reach x + y = y + x?", "a": "no: a world with two extra members breaks it", "w": ["yes: by rung 4", "yes: by self-sameness", "it cannot be told"], "why": "True-everywhere: a world where it fails shows it is not reached."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38d"]=[{"q": "If every clash-free collection has a world, what is true in every world of G is reached. Which technique?", "a": "two-questions", "w": ["full-growth", "term-world", "true-everywhere"], "why": "If G ⊬ φ, G with ¬φ is clash-free, and its world makes φ false."}, {"q": "A clash-free collection grows into one holding each statement or its denial, with named examples. Which technique?", "a": "full-growth", "w": ["term-world", "finite-parts", "bigger-world"], "why": "Round by round along the listing."}, {"q": "In the world of names, a statement is true exactly when it is in G*. Which technique?", "a": "term-world", "w": ["full-growth", "two-questions", "matching-worlds"], "why": "Proved by building, using fullness at ¬ and the named examples at ∃."}, {"q": "In a listed language, whatever is true in every world of G is reached from G. Which technique?", "a": "reached-if-true-everywhere", "w": ["true-everywhere", "two-questions", "search-reached"], "why": "Two-questions, with full-growth and term-world."}, {"q": "Every finite part of a collection has a world. A line reads “so the whole has one”. Which technique?", "a": "finite-parts", "w": ["countable-world", "full-growth", "proof-check"], "why": "A clash would use only finitely many givens."}, {"q": "Does the growth of 38d2 spend the axiom of choice?", "a": "no: the listing and a settled technique fix each round", "w": ["yes: at every round", "yes: at the named examples", "only for countable languages"], "why": "4d’s recursion builds the rounds."}, {"q": "Which line of the two-questions technique spends not-not out?", "a": "“G with ¬φ clashes, so G reaches φ”", "w": ["“G has a world”", "“φ is in G*”", "“the language is listed”"], "why": "Refute gives ¬¬φ; not-not out gives φ."}, {"q": "Can a collection have exactly the finite worlds as its worlds?", "a": "no: add “at least n members” for each n", "w": ["yes: “the world is finite”", "yes: the empty collection", "only with ⊥"], "why": "Finite-parts gives the whole an endless world."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38e"]=[{"q": "A collection in a listed language has an endless world. A line reads “so it has a listed endless one”. Which technique?", "a": "countable-world", "w": ["bigger-world", "listed-line", "finite-parts"], "why": "The world of names has listed terms as members."}, {"q": "A collection with an endless world has a world with as many members as any set X. Which technique?", "a": "bigger-world", "w": ["countable-world", "seen-from-outside", "full-growth"], "why": "Add a name for each member of X, all different, and use finite-parts."}, {"q": "Which technique spends choice?", "a": "bigger-world", "w": ["countable-world", "listed-line", "term-world"], "why": "A language with a name for each member of X must be well-ordered, by 6e."}, {"q": "A listed world of the true statements of the line has a bounded set with no least upper bound. Which technique?", "a": "listed-line", "w": ["countable-world", "nearest-real", "seen-from-outside"], "why": "35b’s diagonal, run inside the listed world."}, {"q": "Is the least upper bound one of the statements of the line?", "a": "no: it speaks of every bounded set, not of members", "w": ["yes", "yes, as an endless list", "only in ℚ"], "why": "A formula’s ∀ runs over members only."}, {"q": "In a listed world of the axioms for sets, the world says its reals cannot be listed. Is that a clash?", "a": "no: the listing from outside is not a member of the world", "w": ["yes", "yes: the axioms clash", "no: the reals are finite there"], "why": "The seen-from-outside technique."}, {"q": "Two copies of the fractions glued one after the other, with <. Matched with the fractions?", "a": "yes", "w": ["no: it is twice as long", "no: it has a gap", "only if listed differently"], "why": "It is listed, dense and without ends."}, {"q": "Does a listed world of the true statements of the line show that the line can be listed?", "a": "no: it is a different world, with holes", "w": ["yes", "yes, from outside", "only with choice"], "why": "R is not that world."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38f"]=[{"q": "Some world of arithmetic holds a member past every numeral. Which technique?", "a": "past-every-numeral", "w": ["block", "numerals-unpicked", "countable-world"], "why": "Add c with c > n̄ for each n; finite parts have N as a world."}, {"q": "Below every block past the numerals there is another, and above each another. Which technique?", "a": "block", "w": ["past-every-numeral", "nearest-real", "shadow-slope"], "why": "Halve, and double, carrying even-or-odd into the world."}, {"q": "No formula of arithmetic picks out exactly the values of the numerals in such a world. Which technique?", "a": "numerals-unpicked", "w": ["block", "undefinable-truth", "one-reading"], "why": "Induction would make that part the whole world."}, {"q": "Every finite member of *R is infinitely close to exactly one real. Which technique?", "a": "nearest-real", "w": ["shadow-slope", "block", "listed-line"], "why": "The least upper bound of the reals below it."}, {"q": "The ratio (f(x + ε) − f(x))/ε is infinitely close to f′(x). Which technique?", "a": "shadow-slope", "w": ["nearest-real", "past-every-numeral", "block"], "why": "Carry the slope’s definition into *R."}, {"q": "What is the nearest real of ((x + ε)³ − x³)/ε, for x a real?", "a": "3x²", "w": ["3x² + 3xε", "0", "x³"], "why": "3x² + 3xε + ε², and the last two are infinitely small."}, {"q": "Is 1/ε finite?", "a": "no: it is greater than every real", "w": ["yes", "yes: its nearest real is 0", "it is not a member"], "why": "ε < 1/r for every positive real r."}, {"q": "Is ε the smallest positive member of *R?", "a": "no: ε/2 is smaller", "w": ["yes", "yes, by definition", "no: 0 is"], "why": "Halving carries into *R."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38g"]=[{"q": "Different strings get different codes, and a string can be read back. Which technique?", "a": "prime-code", "w": ["list-in-one-number", "search-reached", "one-reading"], "why": "One prime list for each number (4j)."}, {"q": "What is ⌜0 = 0⌝?", "a": "2430", "w": ["5", "1 · 5 · 1", "2 · 3 · 5"], "why": "2¹ · 3⁵ · 5¹."}, {"q": "Every finite list is read off as remainders of one number u by 1 + (i + 1)v. Which technique?", "a": "list-in-one-number", "w": ["prime-code", "pair-code", "search-reached"], "why": "Dividers sharing no prime, then 4j’s remainder argument."}, {"q": "For the list 2, 0, 3, what are v and the dividers?", "a": "v = 24; 25, 49, 73", "w": ["v = 6; 7, 13, 19", "v = 3; 4, 7, 10", "v = 24; 24, 48, 72"], "why": "m = 4, v = 4!, dᵢ = 1 + (i + 1)v."}, {"q": "Every search statement true in N is reached by PA. Which technique?", "a": "search-reached", "w": ["true-everywhere", "proof-listing", "unreached-truth"], "why": "Find the witness and check it."}, {"q": "PA reaches a search statement. A line reads “so it is true in N”. Which technique?", "a": "true-everywhere", "w": ["search-reached", "proof-check", "prime-code"], "why": "N is a world of PA."}, {"q": "Without the induction line, which fails to be reached?", "a": "x + y = y + x", "w": ["0 + 0 = 0", "S0 ≠ 0", "S0 + 0 = S0"], "why": "A world with two extra members makes it false."}, {"q": "Is 0 = 0 a statement about 2430?", "a": "no: 2430 is its code", "w": ["yes", "yes, in PA", "only in N"], "why": "The statement speaks of 0."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38h"]=[{"q": "If PA reaches φ, PA reaches Prov(⌜φ⌝). Which technique?", "a": "provable-mark", "w": ["self-statement", "search-reached", "no-self-certificate"], "why": "The proof’s code is the witness; search-reached does the rest."}, {"q": "For every ψ(x₀) there is δ with PA ⊢ δ ⇔ ψ(⌜δ⌝). Which technique?", "a": "self-statement", "w": ["provable-mark", "unreached-truth", "undefinable-truth"], "why": "δ is D applied to the numeral of D’s own code."}, {"q": "Which line does the provable-mark technique hold in trust?", "a": "that Proof is a formula of arithmetic", "w": ["that PA is true in N", "that U is true", "that PA clashes"], "why": "Rung 39 makes it good."}, {"q": "Why can δ not hold the numeral of its own code?", "a": "the numeral of n has n + 1 marks, more than δ", "w": ["codes are secret", "numerals are not terms", "δ has no placeholder"], "why": "A code is far larger than the string’s length."}, {"q": "Is Prov(x) a bounded formula?", "a": "no: its some over proofs is unbounded", "w": ["yes", "yes, below x", "it is not a formula"], "why": "No bound on the size of a proof."}, {"q": "δ₃ says…", "a": "that it is not reached by PA", "w": ["that it is false", "that it is reached", "that its code is even"], "why": "PA ⊢ δ₃ ⇔ ¬Prov(⌜δ₃⌝)."}, {"q": "How does δ₃ differ from 1b.4.4’s statement?", "a": "it says “not reached”, not “not so”", "w": ["it does not differ", "it is about the line", "it is false"], "why": "Truth and reaching differ, so δ₃ need not clash."}, {"q": "ψ has no free placeholder. Which δ works?", "a": "ψ itself", "w": ["¬ψ", "⊥", "none"], "why": "PA ⊢ ψ ⇔ ψ."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38i"]=[{"q": "U is true in N, and PA reaches neither U nor ¬U. Which technique?", "a": "unreached-truth", "w": ["every-list", "no-self-certificate", "undefinable-truth"], "why": "A proof of U would give Prov(⌜U⌝) and ¬Prov(⌜U⌝)."}, {"q": "T holds PA, is settled by a recipe and is true in N. A line reads “so some truth is not reached by T”. Which technique?", "a": "every-list", "w": ["unreached-truth", "proof-listing", "undefinable-truth"], "why": "Build T’s own unreached statement."}, {"q": "Which fact does “PA does not reach U” spend?", "a": "PA is clash-free", "w": ["PA is complete", "U is false", "PA can be listed"], "why": "Else PA reaches both Prov(⌜U⌝) and its denial."}, {"q": "Can the true statements of arithmetic be listed by a recipe?", "a": "no", "w": ["yes", "yes, slowly", "only the search statements cannot"], "why": "Their T would break the every-list technique."}, {"q": "In a world of PA where U is false, the member e codes a proof of U in the world’s eyes. Where is e?", "a": "past every numeral", "w": ["at 0", "at a numeral", "nowhere"], "why": "No number codes a proof of U."}, {"q": "PA does not reach U. Is U false?", "a": "no: U is true in N", "w": ["yes", "neither", "false in N, true elsewhere"], "why": "Not reached is not false."}, {"q": "U is added to PA. Does the new collection reach every truth?", "a": "no: it has its own unreached statement", "w": ["yes", "yes, with Con too", "only for search statements"], "why": "Every-list applies again."}, {"q": "Which weaker fact than “PA is true in N” keeps ¬U unreached?", "a": "that PA never reaches ∃y θ(y) with every ¬θ(n̄)", "w": ["that PA is finite", "that PA is clash-free", "that U is short"], "why": "This is ω-consistency, the name in the basement."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38j"]=[{"q": "If PA is clash-free, PA does not reach Con. Which technique?", "a": "no-self-certificate", "w": ["unreached-truth", "every-list", "provable-mark"], "why": "37i1 carried inside gives PA ⊢ Con ⇒ U."}, {"q": "What does Con say?", "a": "PA does not reach ⊥", "w": ["PA reaches every truth", "PA is true", "U is true"], "why": "Con is ¬Prov(⌜⊥⌝)."}, {"q": "Which fact about □ is the provable-mark technique itself?", "a": "if PA ⊢ φ then PA ⊢ □φ", "w": ["□(φ ⇒ ψ) ⇒ (□φ ⇒ □ψ)", "□φ ⇒ □□φ", "□φ ⇒ φ"], "why": "The first fact."}, {"q": "PA ⊢ □U ⇒ □□U uses which fact?", "a": "the third", "w": ["the first", "the second", "none"], "why": "The provable-mark technique proved inside PA."}, {"q": "Is PA with ¬Con clash-free?", "a": "yes, if PA is", "w": ["no", "no: ¬Con is false", "it cannot be told"], "why": "PA does not reach Con."}, {"q": "PA ⊢ □φ ⇒ φ for some φ. What follows?", "a": "PA ⊢ φ", "w": ["PA ⊢ ¬φ", "PA clashes", "nothing"], "why": "Löb’s result, 38j.6.1."}, {"q": "A collection holding PA, settled by a recipe, reaches its own Con. What follows?", "a": "it clashes", "w": ["it is true", "it is complete", "nothing"], "why": "The technique read the other way."}, {"q": "Does PA ⊬ Con mean that PA might clash?", "a": "no: PA is true in N", "w": ["yes", "yes, in some world", "it cannot be told"], "why": "Unprovable is not false."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38k"]=[{"q": "No formula of arithmetic is a truth formula for arithmetic. Which technique?", "a": "undefinable-truth", "w": ["unreached-truth", "numerals-unpicked", "no-self-certificate"], "why": "Diagonal on ¬True gives a liar, and a clash."}, {"q": "Which formula does the proof feed to the diagonal?", "a": "¬True(x)", "w": ["¬Prov(x)", "Prov(x)", "x = x"], "why": "λ ⇔ ¬True(⌜λ⌝)."}, {"q": "For which statements is there a truth formula?", "a": "the bounded ones", "w": ["all of them", "none", "the ones with an unbounded every"], "why": "A search that stops settles each."}, {"q": "Where is truth in N defined?", "a": "in the language of sets", "w": ["in arithmetic", "nowhere", "in PA"], "why": "4d’s recursion builds the truth function as one set."}, {"q": "Is Prov(x) a truth formula?", "a": "no: U is true and not reached", "w": ["yes", "yes, for PA", "only for codes"], "why": "Reached and true are different collections."}, {"q": "Does the undefinable-truth technique make “true in N” meaningless?", "a": "no: it is defined in a richer language", "w": ["yes", "yes, for U", "only for sets"], "why": "Arithmetic’s own formulas cannot define it."}, {"q": "A partial truth formula for statements with at most n everys and somes. Does the proof run against it?", "a": "no: λ has more than n", "w": ["yes", "yes, with a clash", "only for n = 0"], "why": "λ carries the truth formula’s own everys and somes."}, {"q": "Which answer to the liar does the technique support most directly?", "a": "levels: each language defines the truth of a smaller one", "w": ["both true and false", "the liar is true", "none"], "why": "It shows what arithmetic cannot do, and refutes no other logic."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38l"]=[{"q": "Every world of PA₂ is matched with N. Which technique?", "a": "only-the-numbers", "w": ["no-full-calculus", "term-world", "matching-worlds"], "why": "Second-order induction catches every member as a numeral’s value."}, {"q": "No recipe lists exactly the second-order statements true in every world. Which technique?", "a": "no-full-calculus", "w": ["only-the-numbers", "every-list", "proof-listing"], "why": "Else the truths of N would be listed."}, {"q": "Over what does ∀X run in a world?", "a": "every part of the world", "w": ["the parts a formula picks out", "the members", "the finite parts"], "why": "2g’s set of all subsets."}, {"q": "Why is second-order induction false in 38f’s world?", "a": "the numeral values hold 0, are closed under next, and are not everything", "w": ["induction fails for some formula", "the world is finite", "next is not one-to-one"], "why": "No formula picks that part out."}, {"q": "Does the finite-parts technique hold for second-order statements?", "a": "no", "w": ["yes", "yes, for PA₂", "only for the line"], "why": "PA₂ with all demands n̄ < c: each finite part has a world, the whole none."}, {"q": "PA₂ has only N as a world. Does it reach every truth of arithmetic?", "a": "no: no calculus reaches all its truths", "w": ["yes", "yes, by 38d", "only the search statements"], "why": "One world, and still no recipe."}, {"q": "Read ∀X over only the parts a formula picks out. Which technique fails?", "a": "only-the-numbers", "w": ["finite-parts", "reached-if-true-everywhere", "one-reading"], "why": "37d’s techniques come back."}, {"q": "Which first-order fact does a second-order “the world is finite” break?", "a": "finite-parts", "w": ["one-reading", "proof-check", "true-everywhere"], "why": "With “at least n” for each n, every finite part has a world."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38m"]=[{"q": "To code a string, write each mark’s number as the power of the next prime and multiply. Which technique?", "a": "code-by-primes", "w": ["code-reading", "pair-code", "prime-code"], "why": "37m1."}, {"q": "Divide by 2 as often as it goes, then by 3, and on; the counts are the marks. Which technique?", "a": "code-reading", "w": ["code-by-primes", "code-test", "pair-code"], "why": "The string ends at the first prime that does not divide."}, {"q": "A number codes a formula exactly when its primes run in order and the string read back is a formula. Which technique?", "a": "code-test", "w": ["code-reading", "one-reading", "prime-code"], "why": "Each check stops."}, {"q": "⟨x, y⟩ = (x + y)(x + y + 1)/2 + y. Which technique?", "a": "pair-code", "w": ["code-by-primes", "list-in-one-number", "code-test"], "why": "35a’s diagonal listing, counted."}, {"q": "What is ⟨3, 5⟩?", "a": "41", "w": ["39", "15", "8"], "why": "8 · 9/2 + 5."}, {"q": "What does 12 code?", "a": "S0, a term", "w": ["0 = 0", "nothing", "x₀"], "why": "2² · 3¹: marks S, 0."}, {"q": "What does 10 code?", "a": "no string: 3 is skipped", "w": ["00", "S", "0 0 0"], "why": "10 = 2 · 5."}, {"q": "Which pair has the number 100?", "a": "(4, 9)", "w": ["(9, 4)", "(10, 0)", "(5, 8)"], "why": "w = 13, y = 100 − 91 = 9, x = 13 − 9."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38n"]=[{"q": "Every column can be drawn as a tree with the same foot, and back. Which technique?", "a": "tree-column", "w": ["detour-cut", "swapped-order", "derived-move"], "why": "Read the column from the foot up."}, {"q": "∃x ∀y R(x, y) ⊢ ∀y ∃x R(x, y) as a tree. Which technique?", "a": "swapped-order", "w": ["tree-column", "detour-cut", "true-everywhere"], "why": "2b’s licensed swap, drawn."}, {"q": "A tree makes a mark and at once uses it; the pair is cut out. Which technique?", "a": "detour-cut", "w": ["tree-column", "derived-move", "swapped-order"], "why": "The foot and the givens stay."}, {"q": "How is a dropped supposition marked in a tree?", "a": "in brackets with a number, the bar carrying the same number", "w": ["crossed out", "at the foot", "not at all"], "why": "[P]¹ and the bar marked 1."}, {"q": "Which bar fails when the tree tries ∀y ∃x R(x, y) ⊢ ∃x ∀y R(x, y)?", "a": "speak of everything, with v free in an open leaf", "w": ["show an example", "use an every", "join"], "why": "The leaf [R(w, v)] blocks it."}, {"q": "Which two moves reach a foot unrelated to the leaves?", "a": "prove an if with an unused supposition, and everything permitted", "w": ["join and keep left", "use an every and show an example", "swap and self-sameness"], "why": "37n4."}, {"q": "Can a tree with no ⊥ reach an unrelated foot?", "a": "yes: add an option, then the remaining option", "w": ["no", "only with a free if", "only in a clash"], "why": "The third tree of 38n4."}, {"q": "A logic refuses the free if and everything permitted, and wants to block the third tree. What else must it refuse?", "a": "one of add an option, the remaining option, or chaining proofs", "w": ["join", "self-sameness", "nothing"], "why": "Such logics are relevance logics."}];
(window.LADDER_CARDS=window.LADDER_CARDS||{})["38·cb29"]=[{"q": "At every turn of the full game the label falls. Which technique?", "a": "label-falls", "w": ["full-game", "falling-run", "base-bump"], "why": "A bump keeps the label; taking 1 lowers it."}, {"q": "Every full game ends. Which technique?", "a": "full-game", "w": ["label-falls", "falling-run", "search-reached"], "why": "Falling labels, and the falling-run technique."}, {"q": "A run of points, each a member of the one before, stops. Which technique finishes the full-game proof?", "a": "falling-run", "w": ["label-falls", "base-bump", "Replace"], "why": "29·cb3.4."}, {"q": "What is 4 in full base 2, and its label?", "a": "2², label ω^ω", "w": ["2 · 2, label ω · 2", "4, label 4", "2² , label ω²"], "why": "The exponent 2 is itself written as the base."}, {"q": "From 4, the full game runs…", "a": "4, 26, 41, 60, 83, …", "w": ["4, 3, 2, 1, 0", "4, 8, 16, 32", "4, 26, 25, 24"], "why": "Bump everywhere, take 1."}, {"q": "Does PA reach E, that every full game ends?", "a": "no", "w": ["yes", "yes, game by game", "it is false"], "why": "PA ⊢ E ⇒ Con, held in trust, and no-self-certificate."}, {"q": "Does PA reach that the game from 4 ends?", "a": "yes", "w": ["no", "it does not end", "only with Con"], "why": "A true search statement (search-reached)."}, {"q": "How does E differ from U?", "a": "E says nothing about proofs or codes", "w": ["E is false", "E is about sets", "E is reached"], "why": "It is about bases and taking 1 away."}];
/* phase F und_r38: understanding cards, appended (ids follow the deck's own) */
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38a1.1","q":"How are the terms and formulas of a language built, what does the one-reading technique say, and why does a definition by recursion on formulas depend on it?","a":"Terms: every placeholder and name, and f(t₁, …, tₙ) from terms; formulas: t = s, R(t₁, …, tₙ) and ⊥, then ¬φ, (φ ∧ ψ), (φ ∨ ψ), (φ ⇒ ψ), ∀x φ and ∃x φ, and nothing else; every formula is built in exactly one way; a recursion defines a thing one way of building at a time, so it is defined once only if there is one way","w":["Terms are the names only; formulas are any strings of marks; every formula has at least one reading; recursion needs no such thing","Terms and formulas are built by the same clauses; every formula is built in exactly one way; recursion needs it only to keep formulas short","Terms are built from placeholders and names by function marks; formulas from atoms by joining words and every and some; a formula may be built in several ways, and recursion picks the first"],"why":"A formula is a string with one history."},
 {"u":"38a2.1","q":"What does a world give each kind of mark of its language, what is an assignment, and how is a term’s value found?","a":"A set with at least one member, with a member for each name, a function on lists of n members for each n-place function mark, and a set of lists of n members for each n-place relation mark; an assignment gives each placeholder a member; the value goes by recursion: a placeholder takes its assigned member, a name its member, and f(t₁, …, tₙ) the meaning of f at the values of the parts","w":["A truth value for each mark; an assignment gives each formula a truth value; a term’s value is its length","A member for each name and for each function mark; an assignment gives each name a placeholder; the value of a term is the string itself","A set of members and a meaning for each mark; an assignment gives each placeholder a member; the value of a term is the same in every world"],"why":"The term is the same string; its value belongs to the world."},
 {"u":"38a3.1","q":"When is ∀x φ true in a world under an assignment s, what does the free-places technique say, and why can a statement then be true or false in a world outright?","a":"When φ is true under every assignment that agrees with s except perhaps at x; whether a formula is true depends only on what the assignment gives its free placeholders; a statement has none, so every assignment gives it the same truth","w":["When φ is true for every name of the language; truth depends on every placeholder; a statement is true outright only in N","When φ is true under some assignment that differs from s at x; truth depends only on the free placeholders; a statement needs one fixed assignment","When a recipe has checked φ at every member; truth depends only on the bound placeholders; a statement is true in every world or none"],"why":"Truth in N is defined, not computed: no recipe checks every number."},
 {"u":"38a4.1","q":"What does G ⊨ φ say, what does the matching-worlds technique say, and where does its proof need the matching to be onto?","a":"φ is true in every world of G; if a matching carries every mark of M to M′, the two worlds make exactly the same statements true; onto is spent at the every clause: each member of M′ is h(u) for some u, so changing the carried assignment at x runs over every member of M′","w":["Some proof from G reaches φ; matched worlds may differ on statements with every; onto is spent at the atoms","φ is true in every world of G; matched worlds make the same statements true; onto is spent at t = t′, where one-to-one would not do","φ is true in some world of G; any two worlds of G make the same statements true; onto is never needed"],"why":"N and the integers are unmatched and differ on ∃x₀ (S(x₀) = 0)."},
 {"u":"38a5.1","q":"Why is ∃x₀ φ not true in M just when φ holds of some name, and why is ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) not false in every world?","a":"Some runs over the members, and a world may have members no name reaches; and ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) is true in a world with one member: truth belongs to a world","w":["Some runs over the names, but names may repeat; ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) is false in every world","Some runs over the formulas; ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) is true in N","Some runs over the members, and every member has a name; ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0) is neither true nor false"],"why":"The line has members no name reaches (35b)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38b1.1","q":"Which moves make up the calculus, what does G ⊢ φ say, what does the derived-move technique add, and on which earlier technique does its proof lean?","a":"One pair for each mark, a move that makes it and a move that uses it, for =, ∧, ∨, ⇒, ¬, ∀ and ∃, with everything permitted for ⊥ and not-not out; G ⊢ φ: some proof from G has the foot (∅, φ); a move reached once by a proof may be used as one line, and a proof using it can be rewritten without it; the rewriting leans on the more-suppositions technique (4a14)","w":["Any move that keeps truth in N; G ⊢ φ: φ is true in every world of G; derived moves are new axioms; it leans on induction alone","One pair for each mark; G ⊢ φ: some proof from G has the foot (∅, φ); a derived move may be used only once in a proof; it leans on the one-reading technique","The twelve techniques of 5d; G ⊢ φ: G holds φ; derived moves cannot be removed from a proof; it leans on not-not out"],"why":"Turn-and-deny is reached once, so the calculus may cite it in one line."},
 {"u":"38b2.1","q":"Which conditions do speak of everything and use a some carry, and what could the calculus reach without the first?","a":"Speak of everything may go from φ(y) to ∀x φ(x) only when y is free in no supposition and in no given used above; use a some needs its y free nowhere else, not in S, R or ∃x φ(x); without the first it would reach ∀x₀ (x₀ = 0), false in N","w":["Both need y to be a name; without the first the calculus would reach nothing new","Speak of everything needs y free in every supposition; use a some needs y free in R; without the first it would reach 0 = 0","Speak of everything needs y free in no supposition; use a some carries no condition; without the first it would only reach true statements more slowly"],"why":"A case is “anything” when the argument used nothing about it."},
 {"u":"38b3.1","q":"What does the proof-check technique say and why does its recipe stop, what does the proof-listing technique give, and what does the listing never tell?","a":"Whether a finite list of lines is a proof from a finite G is settled by a recipe that always stops: each line needs finitely many tries, each comparing finitely many marks, and the count of lines left falls by one a round; if G can be listed, its proofs and the statements they reach can be listed one after another; if no proof reaches φ, the listing runs for ever and never says so","w":["A recipe finds a proof of any true statement; it stops because proofs are short; the listing says no after finitely many rounds","A recipe checks a finite list and always stops; the proofs from G can be listed; when φ is never reached the listing stops and reports it","A recipe checks a proof only if G is empty; the proofs cannot be listed; the listing tells nothing at all"],"why":"Every proof turns up at some round, since it uses finitely many givens."},
 {"u":"38b4.1","q":"Why may ∀x₀ ∃x₁ ¬(x₀ = x₁) not give ∃x₁ ¬(x₁ = x₁) by use an every, and why does G ⊢ φ not mean that φ is true?","a":"The term x₁ put in for x₀ is caught by the some that binds x₁: rename the bound placeholder first; and ⊢ is about lists of lines while truth, ⊨, is about worlds, joined only by 38c and 38d","w":["Use an every may put in names only; and G ⊢ φ does mean φ is true in N","The term x₁ is caught by the some; and G ⊢ φ means φ is true in every world whatever G is","Nothing forbids it; and G ⊢ φ means φ is false"],"why":"A term put in must not be caught."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38c1.1","q":"When does a line (S, R) hold in a world, what does the move-keeps technique say, and where is speak of everything’s fresh-name condition spent?","a":"When every assignment that makes every formula of S true makes R true; each move, fed lines that hold in a world, writes a line that holds there; the condition is spent where s is changed at y to any member: S stays true only because y is free in no formula of S","w":["When R is in S; each move writes a line true in N; the condition is never spent","When some assignment makes S and R true; each move keeps lines that hold; the condition is spent at swap","When every assignment makes R true; moves keep truth only for statements; the condition is spent to make R shorter"],"why":"Without it, changing s at y could make S false."},
 {"u":"38c2.1","q":"What does the true-everywhere technique say and how is it proved, what is a clash-free collection, and why is a collection with a world clash-free?","a":"If G reaches φ then φ is true in every world of G; by induction on line numbers every line of the proof holds in the world, the moved ones by move-keeps; G is clash-free when it does not reach ⊥; if G reached ⊥, ⊥ would be true in a world of G, and it is true in none","w":["If φ is true in every world of G then G reaches φ; by listing proofs; clash-free means G is true in N; a world proves nothing about clashes","If G reaches φ then φ is true in every world of G; by checking N alone; clash-free means G holds no denial; a collection with a world may still reach ⊥","If G reaches φ then φ is true in some world; by cases; clash-free means G is finite; a finite collection has a world"],"why":"G ⊢ φ gives G ⊨ φ."},
 {"u":"38c3.1","q":"How does a world show that G does not reach φ, which earlier arguments of the ladder leaned on this before it was proved, and what can a world never show?","a":"If some world of G makes φ false, G does not reach φ: the true-everywhere technique turned round; 3f’s world for infinity, 6d’s two worlds for choice, 29c’s disc plane and 29·cb3.2’s world past the run all leaned on it; a world cannot show that a statement is reached","w":["A world in which φ is true shows G does not reach φ; only 29c used it; a world can show anything","A world of G making φ false shows G does not reach φ; no earlier argument used it; a world can also show that φ is reached","A world with no members shows it; 4c’s induction used it; a world can never show a statement false"],"why":"The six matchings of {1, 2, 3}: the techniques for moves do not reach turn-about for ∘."},
 {"u":"38c4.1","q":"Why are “every box reaches φ” and “every world of G makes φ true” one property, and which half does this page prove and which does 38d?","a":"For a listed language G ⊢ φ exactly when G ⊨ φ: this page proves that what is reached is true in every world, the true-everywhere technique, and 38d proves that what is true in every world is reached","w":["They are the same by definition, so nothing is proved on either page","This page proves that what is true everywhere is reached; 38d proves the easy half","They agree only for statements without every; 38d shows they differ in general"],"why":"A statement not reached must fail in some world."},
 {"u":"38c5.1","q":"Why does “φ is true in N” give no proof of φ from the axioms of arithmetic, and why does G ⊢ φ make φ true only in the worlds of G?","a":"That runs the technique backwards: reached gives true in every world of the axioms, and true in the one world N does not give reached; and the proof may use the givens, which need not be true elsewhere, so φ may be false in a world that is not a world of G","w":["It does give a proof, by the proof-listing technique; and G ⊢ φ makes φ true in every world whatever","True in N gives reached only for short statements; and G ⊢ φ makes φ true in N alone","N is not a world; and G ⊢ φ says nothing about worlds"],"why":"38i shows that true does not give reached."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38d1.1","q":"What does the two-questions technique say, which line spends not-not out, and what single claim does the way back then rest on?","a":"If every clash-free collection has a world, then whatever is true in every world of G is reached from G; the line that takes G ∪ {¬φ} ⊢ ⊥ to G ⊢ ¬¬φ and on to G ⊢ φ spends it; the rest hangs on: a collection that does not clash has a world","w":["If G has a world then G reaches everything; no line spends not-not out; the rest hangs on choice","If every clash-free collection has a world, what is true in every world of G is reached; the last line spends not-not out; the rest hangs on N being a world","If every collection has a world, nothing is reached; the first line spends it; the rest hangs on the proof-check technique"],"why":"If G does not reach φ, G with ¬φ is clash-free and has a world where φ is false."},
 {"u":"38d2.1","q":"How is G grown round by round, what does the full-growth technique say, and why is no axiom of choice spent?","a":"Add new names, list every statement, and at round n put in φₙ if that keeps the collection clash-free and ¬φₙ if not, with ψ(c) for a fresh name c whenever ∃x ψ(x) goes in; a clash-free collection in a listed language grows into a clash-free one holding each statement or its denial and a named example of each some; the listing fixes the order and a settled condition fixes each round, so 4d’s recursion builds it","w":["Put in every statement and its denial; the grown collection is full but clashes; choice is spent at every round","At round n put in φₙ if it keeps the collection clash-free, else ¬φₙ; the grown collection is clash-free, full and named; choice is spent, since each round picks","At round n put in φₙ if it is true in N; the grown collection is the truths of N; no choice is spent because N is listed"],"why":"The test at each round is no recipe, but it is a condition."},
 {"u":"38d3.1","q":"What are the members of the world of names and what do the marks mean there, what does the term-world technique say, and what does the reached-if-true-everywhere technique then give?","a":"Classes of terms with no placeholders, two terms kin when t = t′ is in G*; a name means its own class, f([t₁], …) = [f(t₁, …)], and R holds of classes when R(t₁, …) is in G*; a statement is true in the world of names exactly when it is in G*; so every clash-free collection in a listed language has a world, and G ⊨ φ gives G ⊢ φ","w":["The numbers; each name means its place in the list; a statement is true there when it is true in N; so G ⊢ φ gives G ⊨ φ","The terms themselves, one member each; a name means itself; a statement is true there when G* reaches its denial; so nothing follows","Classes of kin terms; a name means its class; a statement is true there exactly when it is in G*; but the world is a world of G* only, never of G"],"why":"Forget the new names, and M* is a world for G’s own language."},
 {"u":"38d4.1","q":"What does the finite-parts technique say, on which fact about proofs does it rest, and why can no collection of statements have exactly the finite worlds as its worlds?","a":"If every finite part of a collection has a world, the whole has one; a proof is finite, so a clash would be reached from a finite part; a collection with worlds of every finite size, joined with “at least n members” for every n, has each finite part met, so it has a world, and that world is endless","w":["If the whole has a world, some finite part has; proofs can be listed; a collection can have exactly the finite worlds, by listing them","If every finite part has a world, the whole has one; a proof is finite; and the world it gives is always finite, so the finite worlds can be picked out","If every finite part is true in N, the whole is true in N; proofs are true in N; no collection has finite worlds at all"],"why":"Its accepted name is compactness, as in 34d: finitely many suffice."},
 {"u":"38d5.1","q":"Why does G ⊬ φ not give G ⊢ ¬φ, and why does “every finite part of G is true in N” not make G true in N?","a":"G may reach neither φ nor ¬φ: only the grown G* holds one of each pair; and the finite-parts technique gives some world of G, not that it is N","w":["G always reaches one of the two; and N is the only world there is","G may reach both; and finite parts are never true in N","G reaches ¬φ by refute; and the technique gives N itself"],"why":"38f builds such a world that is not N."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38e1.1","q":"What does the countable-world technique say, and why can the members of the world of names be listed?","a":"A collection in a listed language that has a world has one whose members can be listed, and if it has an endless world it has one that is endless and can be listed; the members are classes of terms with no placeholder, finite strings of listed marks, so list the classes in the order of their first terms","w":["Every world of such a collection can be listed; the members are numbers","A collection with a world has a listed one; the members can be listed because the world is finite","A collection with an endless world has only worlds the size of the line; the members cannot be listed"],"why":"For an endless one, run the same lines with “at least n members” added for every n."},
 {"u":"38e2.1","q":"What does the bigger-world technique say, which line of its proof spends choice, and why does the countable-world technique spend none?","a":"A collection with an endless world has, for any set X, a world with at least as many members as X; choice is spent where the finite-parts technique is run on a language that cannot be listed, whose statements are lined up by a well-order; the countable-world technique works in a listed language, where the list fixes the order","w":["A collection with an endless world has only listed worlds; no line spends choice","A collection with an endless world has a world at least as large as any X; choice is spent in adding the new names; the countable-world technique spends it too","A collection with a finite world has worlds of every size; choice is spent at the first line; the countable-world technique spends replacement"],"why":"A new name c_u for each u in X, with c_u ≠ c_v for different u and v."},
 {"u":"38e3.1","q":"What does the listed-line technique say, which tools of 35b does its proof run inside C, and why is the least upper bound not among the statements true in both R and C?","a":"A listed world C of the true statements of the line has a bounded set of members with no least upper bound among them; 35b’s nested closed stretches, which spend only order, the field techniques and the least upper bound, would give a member on no place of the list; a formula’s every runs over members, and the least upper bound speaks of every bounded set of members, which no formula of this language can say","w":["C has every least upper bound; 35b’s decimal digits; the least upper bound is a statement true in both","C has a bounded set with no least upper bound; 35b’s nested stretches; the least upper bound is a statement of the language that is false in R","C is finite; the pair-listing technique; the least upper bound is true in C and false in R"],"why":"True of R, false of C, and not a statement of the language."},
 {"u":"38e4.1","q":"What does the seen-from-outside technique say, and why do “p cannot be listed” in M and a listing of p from outside not clash?","a":"If the axioms for sets have a world, they have one whose members can be listed from outside, holding a set that the world says cannot be listed; inside M “cannot be listed” means no member of M is a matching of ω with p, and the outside listing is not a member of M","w":["The axioms for sets have no world; the two do clash, which is why","Such a world exists, and in it 𝒫(ω) can be listed by a member of M; so 35c is false there","Such a world exists; there is no clash because p is empty in M"],"why":"What a world can say is fixed by what is in it."},
 {"u":"38e5.1","q":"Why does a listed world of the true statements of the line not show the line can be listed, and why does the bigger-world technique spend choice?","a":"C is another world, with holes: 35b holds for R itself; and lining up the statements of a language that cannot be listed takes a well-order, which choice gives","w":["C is R, so the line can be listed; and the technique spends no axiom","C is another world; and the technique spends choice to pick the members of X","C has more members than R; and choice is spent to list N"],"why":"6e’s well-ordering lines the statements up."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38f1.1","q":"What is the numeral of n, what does the past-every-numeral technique say, and why is the world it gives not matched with N?","a":"The term with n marks S on 0; some world makes every statement true in N true and holds a member greater than the value of every numeral; a matching that carries the marks sends each numeral’s value to the same numeral’s value, so that member would go to some n and be below a numeral","w":["The number n itself; every world of arithmetic is N; so the world is matched with N","The term with n marks S on 0; some world of the truths of N holds a member past every numeral; it is not matched because it is finite","The term 0 + n; N itself holds a member past every numeral; it is matched with N by the identity"],"why":"Statements cannot always tell unmatched worlds apart: 38a4’s question, answered no."},
 {"u":"38f2.1","q":"What is a block past the numerals and what is its shape, what does the block technique say, and which truth about N does its proof carry into M?","a":"The members a numeral’s value away from one member past the numerals, running both ways without end like the integers; below every block there is another and above every one another; the proof carries over that every number is even or odd, so c = d + d or d + d + 1 with d in a lower block","w":["The numerals themselves, shaped like N; there is a first block and a last; it carries over that 0 is no next","The members a numeral’s value from c, shaped like the integers; there is a first block past the numerals and no last; it carries over induction","The members between two numerals, a finite run; blocks are all alike; it carries over that every number has a next"],"why":"No first block and no last, and between any two another."},
 {"u":"38f3.1","q":"What does the numerals-unpicked technique say, why is induction true in M for every formula, and what does 4b’s sift use that a formula of arithmetic cannot?","a":"No formula of arithmetic is true in M of exactly the values of the numerals; each case of induction is a statement true in N, so it is in T and true in M; 4b’s sift is a condition about every inductive set, and the marks of arithmetic speak of numbers only","w":["Some formula picks out the numerals; induction fails in M; 4b’s sift uses multiplication","No formula picks out the numerals; induction fails in M for that reason; 4b’s sift uses the order","No formula picks out the numerals; induction is true in M; 4b’s sift uses only the marks 0 and S, which a formula can use too"],"why":"A formula true exactly at the numerals would, by induction, be true at c."},
 {"u":"38f4.1","q":"How is *R built and what is an infinitely small member, what do the nearest-real and shadow-slope techniques say, and which step of rung 8 does the first spend?","a":"Add a name ε to the true statements of the line in a rich language, with 0 < ε and ε below every positive real: every finite part is met, so the finite-parts technique gives a world; ε is above 0 and below every positive real; every finite member is infinitely close to exactly one real; if f has a slope at x, (f(x + ε) − f(x))/ε is infinitely close to f′(x); the first spends the least upper bound","w":["Divide every real by 0; ε is 0; every member is a real; the ratio equals f′(x) exactly; it spends nothing","Add ε with 0 < ε below every positive real; ε is the smallest positive member; every finite member is infinitely close to one real; the ratio is infinitely close to f′(x); it spends the nested stretches only for fractions","Take the fractions as a world; ε is 1/n; every member is close to two reals; the ratio is f(x)/ε; it spends 8a’s cuts"],"why":"For x²: the ratio is 2x + ε, and its nearest real is 2x."},
 {"u":"38f5.1","q":"Why does induction holding in M not make every member a numeral’s value, and why is ε not the smallest positive member of *R?","a":"Induction is for formulas, and no formula picks out the numerals; and ε/2 is positive and smaller, since “every positive member has a smaller one” is true in R and so in *R","w":["Induction fails in M; and ε is the smallest, by its definition","Induction reaches every member of every world; and ε/2 is 0","No formula mentions numerals; and *R has no positive members"],"why":"Every statement true of the reals is true in *R."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38g1.1","q":"How is the code of a string of marks made, what does the prime-code technique say, and on which technique of rung 4 does its proof rest?","a":"Each mark has a number, and the code is 2 to the number of the first mark, times 3 to the number of the second, and so on, the kth prime to the number of the kth mark; different strings get different codes, and a string can be read back from its code; it rests on the one-prime-list technique (4j)","w":["The code is the sum of the marks’ numbers; different strings may share a code; it rests on induction (4c)","The code is the product of the marks’ numbers; a string can be read back from its code; it rests on the doubled-sum technique (4k)","The code is 2 to the first mark’s number, times 3 to the second’s, and so on; different strings get different codes; it rests on the pigeonhole technique"],"why":"⌜0 = 0⌝ = 2¹ · 3⁵ · 5¹ = 2430."},
 {"u":"38g2.1","q":"What are the givens of PA, why does PA reach only statements true in N, and what breaks if the induction line is left out?","a":"0 is nobody’s next; different numbers have different nexts; the two lines for adding and the two for multiplying; and induction, one statement for each formula; every member is true in N, so N is a world of PA and the true-everywhere technique applies; without induction a world with two extra members makes the six lines true and ∀x (0 + x = x) false, so the six never reach it","w":["Every statement true in N; PA reaches only truths because it is finite; without induction nothing breaks","The six lines and induction; PA reaches only truths because every proof is checked by a recipe; without induction PA clashes","The six lines without induction; PA reaches every truth of N; adding induction lets it reach false statements"],"why":"With induction, ∀x (0 + x = x) takes four lines."},
 {"u":"38g3.1","q":"What does the list-in-one-number technique say, why does arithmetic need it, and how does it turn “y is 2 to the power x” into a formula?","a":"For every finite list of numbers there are u and v with the entry in place i equal to the remainder of u on division by 1 + (i + 1) · v; the language has + and · and no mark for powers, yet codes are products of prime powers; “y = 2 to the x” says some u and v store the list 1, 2, 4, …, y, each entry twice the one before, with x + 1 entries","w":["Every list of numbers is one prime power; arithmetic needs it to add; “y = 2 to the x” is written with a new power mark","For every finite list there are u and v storing it by remainders; arithmetic needs it because it has no mark for adding; “y = 2 to the x” says y is even","Every endless list is stored in one number; arithmetic needs it for induction; “y = 2 to the x” cannot be a formula"],"why":"The same trick writes “y is the code of a proof of the formula with code x”."},
 {"u":"38g4.1","q":"What are a bounded formula and a search statement, what does the search-reached technique say, and why is it silent about false search statements?","a":"Bounded: each every and some is kept below a term; a search statement is a bounded formula with somes in front; every search statement true in N is reached by PA, since a true search has an example and the example is a numeral PA can work with; to deny a false one PA would have to deal with every number, which the technique does not cover","w":["Bounded: it has no every; a search statement is any statement with a some; every search statement is reached by PA, true or false","Bounded: every and some kept below a term; a search statement is bounded with somes in front; every statement true in N is reached by PA","Bounded: shorter than a set length; a search statement is one a recipe has checked; false search statements are reached too, as denials"],"why":"“Some number is the code of a proof of φ” has that shape."},
 {"u":"38g5.1","q":"Why is 0 = 0 not a statement about 2430, and why does the search-reached technique not deliver every true statement of arithmetic?","a":"The statement is about 0; 2430 is its code, a number that stands for it as a name stands for a thing; and the technique covers search statements only: an every in front is another matter","w":["It is about 2430, since that is its code; the technique does deliver every truth","The statement is about 0; the technique fails because PA has no induction","Codes are not numbers; the technique covers only false statements"],"why":"38i shows what goes wrong with an every in front."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38h1.1","q":"What do Proof(y, x) and Prov(x) say and what shape has each, what does the provable-mark technique say, and which technique of 38g does its proof use?","a":"Proof(y, x): y is the code of a proof from PA whose foot is the statement with code x, a bounded formula; Prov(x) is ∃y Proof(y, x), “the statement with code x is reached by PA”, a search formula; if PA reaches φ then PA reaches Prov(⌜φ⌝); the proof uses the search-reached technique on the true bounded statement about the proof’s code","w":["Proof(y, x) says x is true; Prov(x) is bounded; if Prov(⌜φ⌝) is true then φ is true; it uses the prime-code technique alone","Proof(y, x) is a search formula and Prov(x) is bounded; if PA reaches φ then φ is true in N; it uses the list-in-one-number technique","Proof(y, x) is bounded and Prov(x) is a search formula; if PA reaches Prov(⌜φ⌝) then PA reaches ¬φ; it uses induction"],"why":"Show an example, with the numeral of the proof’s code."},
 {"u":"38h2.1","q":"What is sub(n), which formula D does the proof build, what does the self-statement technique say, and why need δ not contain its own code?","a":"sub(n) is the code of the statement got by putting the numeral of n into the formula with code n; D(x₀) is ∃y (Sub(x₀, y) ∧ ψ(y)); for every formula ψ(x₀) there is a statement δ with PA reaching δ ⇔ ψ(⌜δ⌝); δ is D fed the numeral of D’s own code, a description, “put this into itself”, whose result is δ","w":["sub(n) is n + 1; D(x₀) is ψ(x₀); for every ψ there is a δ with δ = ψ; δ contains the numeral of its own code","sub(n) is the code of putting n̄ into formula n; D(x₀) is ∃y (Sub(x₀, y) ∧ ψ(y)); some ψ have no such δ; δ holds its own code in its last digit","sub(n) is the code of n’s denial; D is Prov; δ says it is true; δ needs a word for “this statement”"],"why":"The diagonal move of 35b and 2e, with formulas in place of entries."},
 {"u":"38h3.1","q":"What does the diagonal statement for ¬Prov(x) say of itself, and how does that differ from the statement 1b.4.4 refuted?","a":"That it is not reached by PA; 1b.4.4’s statement says of itself that it is not so, and truth and reaching are different, so this one need not clash","w":["That it is false; it is the same statement as 1b.4.4’s","That it is reached; 1b.4.4’s says it is not reached","That its code is even; 1b.4.4’s says its code is odd"],"why":"38i finds out what it is."},
 {"u":"38h4.1","q":"Why can δ not hold the numeral of its own code, and why is Prov(x) not bounded?","a":"That numeral has ⌜δ⌝ marks S, more marks than δ has; and the some of Prov(x) is kept below no term: it is a search formula","w":["Numerals are not allowed in statements; Prov has no some","δ does hold it; Prov(x) is bounded by x","The code of δ is not a number; Prov(x) has two everys"],"why":"δ holds the numeral of n, the code of D."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38i1.1","q":"What is the unreached statement U, why does PA not reach it, and what fact about PA does the last step spend?","a":"The diagonal statement with PA ⊢ U ⇔ ¬Prov(⌜U⌝); if PA reached U it would reach Prov(⌜U⌝) by the provable-mark technique and ¬Prov(⌜U⌝) by U’s own ⇔, a clash; the last step spends that PA is clash-free, since N is a world of it","w":["The statement 0 = 1; PA does not reach it because it is false; it spends induction","The diagonal statement for ¬Prov; PA does not reach it because U is too long to prove; it spends nothing","The diagonal statement for Prov; PA reaches it; it spends that PA is true in N"],"why":"Nothing in the proof looked at what U is about."},
 {"u":"38i2.1","q":"What does the unreached-truth technique say, why is U true, and why is ¬U not reached?","a":"U is true in N, and PA reaches neither U nor ¬U; no number codes a proof of U, so ¬Prov(⌜U⌝) is true in N, and N makes U’s ⇔ true; ¬U is false in N and PA reaches only statements true in N","w":["U is false in N and PA reaches ¬U; U is false because it has no proof","U is true in N and PA reaches it after all; ¬U is not reached because U is","U is neither true nor false; neither is reached because U has no meaning"],"why":"The proof that U is true uses more than PA: that N is a world of PA."},
 {"u":"38i3.1","q":"What does the every-list technique say, which three facts about PA must T share, and why can the true statements of arithmetic not be listed by a recipe?","a":"If T holds PA, membership in T is settled by a recipe, and every member of T is true in N, then some statement true in N is not reached by T; if the truths could be listed, they would be such a T, with membership settled by listing until φ or ¬φ turns up, and it would reach every truth","w":["Every such T reaches every truth; T must be finite, true and short; the truths can be listed","Some truth is not reached by T; T need only be clash-free; the truths cannot be listed because there are uncountably many","Some truth is not reached by T; T must hold PA, be settled by a recipe and be true in N; the truths can be listed, but not proved"],"why":"T with its own unreached statement added is again such a collection."},
 {"u":"38i4.1","q":"In a world of PA where U is false, what does the member e do, and why must it lie past every numeral?","a":"e is, in that world’s eyes, the code of a proof of U; if e were the value of a numeral, Proof of that numeral and ⌜U⌝ would be a true bounded statement in N, and no number codes a proof of U","w":["e is the code of U itself; it is past every numeral because codes are large","e is a real proof of U; it is the value of a numeral","e is the least member; it is past every numeral because U is false"],"why":"U is true in N and false in M, and both are worlds of PA."},
 {"u":"38i5.1","q":"Why does PA ⊬ U not make U false, and why does PA with U added not reach every truth of arithmetic?","a":"U is true in N: not reached is not false; and the every-list technique gives PA + U its own unreached statement","w":["U is false in every world; PA + U does reach every truth","U is true because PA reaches it; PA + U clashes","U has no truth value; PA + U reaches U only"],"why":"U says it is not reached, not that it is false."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38j1.1","q":"What does Con say, is it true in N, and what is 38i1’s argument written in marks?","a":"Con is ¬Prov(⌜⊥⌝): no number is the code of a proof of ⊥ from PA; it is true in N, since PA is clash-free; the argument is Con ⇒ ¬Prov(⌜U⌝), which is Con ⇒ U","w":["Con is Prov(⌜⊥⌝); it is false in N; the argument is U ⇒ Con","Con says PA is true in N; it is true; the argument is Con ⇒ ¬U","Con is ¬Prov(⌜U⌝); it is neither true nor false; the argument is Con ⇔ ⊥"],"why":"If PA could reach that line, Con would be out of reach."},
 {"u":"38j2.1","q":"Which three facts does PA reach about □, which is the provable-mark technique proved inside PA, and which are held in trust?","a":"If PA ⊢ φ then PA ⊢ □φ; PA ⊢ □(φ ⇒ ψ) ⇒ (□φ ⇒ □ψ); and PA ⊢ □φ ⇒ □□φ; the third is the first proved inside PA; the second and the third are held in trust","w":["□φ ⇒ φ; φ ⇒ □φ; □⊥; the first is the provable-mark technique inside; none is held in trust","If PA ⊢ φ then PA ⊢ □φ; □(φ ⇒ ψ) ⇒ (□φ ⇒ □ψ); □φ ⇒ □□φ; the second is the first proved inside PA; only the first is held in trust","□φ ⇔ φ; □¬φ ⇔ ¬□φ; □□φ ⇒ □φ; all three are proved on the page"],"why":"Here □φ is shorthand for Prov(⌜φ⌝)."},
 {"u":"38j3.1","q":"What does the no-self-certificate technique say, which line uses the third fact, and why is a collection that reaches its own Con never to be trusted?","a":"If PA is clash-free, PA does not reach Con; the line PA ⊢ □U ⇒ □□U uses the third fact; a clash-free collection of this kind does not reach its Con, so one that does reach it clashes, and then it reaches everything","w":["PA reaches Con exactly when it is clash-free; the last line uses the third fact; a collection reaching its Con is the safest","If PA is clash-free it does not reach Con; the first line uses the third fact; a collection reaching its Con is merely long","PA reaches ¬Con; no line uses the third fact; such a collection is true in N"],"why":"PA ⊢ Con ⇒ U, and PA does not reach U."},
 {"u":"38j4.1","q":"Why can no proof from the axioms for sets show that they do not clash, if they do not, and what does the world of PA with ¬Con show about clash-free collections?","a":"They hold PA and membership in them is settled by a recipe, so if clash-free they do not reach their own Con; PA with ¬Con does not clash yet reaches a statement false in N, so “does not clash” is weaker than “true of the numbers”","w":["They are too long to check; the world shows that PA clashes","They hold PA, so they reach their own Con easily; the world shows clash-free means true","They have no world; the world shows ¬Con is true in N"],"why":"A proof from a stronger system moves the trust; it does not remove it."},
 {"u":"38j5.1","q":"Why does PA ⊬ Con not mean PA might clash, and why is PA with ¬Con clash-free though ¬Con is false in N?","a":"PA is clash-free, since N is a world of it: PA cannot show it, which is another matter; and ¬Con is false in N but true in another world of PA, and a collection with a world is clash-free","w":["PA might clash, since it cannot prove otherwise; PA with ¬Con clashes","PA is clash-free because it reaches Con; PA with ¬Con is true in N","PA ⊬ Con means Con is false; PA with ¬Con has no world"],"why":"Since PA does not reach Con, PA with ¬Con has a world (38d)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38k1.1","q":"What would a truth formula for arithmetic be, for which statements is there no difficulty, and where does the difficulty begin?","a":"A formula True(x) with N ⊨ True(⌜φ⌝) ⇔ φ for every statement φ of arithmetic; for bounded statements a search formula does it, since each is settled by a search that stops; the trouble starts with everys and somes kept below nothing, whose clause asks about every number at once","w":["A formula true of every code; there is no difficulty for any statement; the trouble is only length","A formula True(x) with N ⊨ True(⌜φ⌝) ⇔ φ; no difficulty for statements with an unbounded every; the trouble starts at t = t′","Prov(x); no difficulty for reached statements; the trouble starts with false ones"],"why":"A table with endlessly many rows is not a number a formula could hold."},
 {"u":"38k2.1","q":"What does the undefinable-truth technique say, which statement does its proof feed to the diagonal, and which supposition does the clash refute?","a":"No formula of arithmetic is a truth formula for arithmetic; the diagonal is run on ψ(x) = ¬True(x), giving λ with λ ⇔ ¬True(⌜λ⌝), and with True a truth formula N would make λ ⇔ ¬λ true; the diagonal is a proved technique, so the supposed truth formula goes","w":["Truth in N means nothing; the diagonal is run on ¬Prov; the clash refutes the diagonal","No formula of arithmetic is a truth formula; the diagonal is run on Prov(x); the clash refutes that PA is clash-free","Some formula is a truth formula; the diagonal is run on True(x); the clash refutes that N is a world"],"why":"The liar of 1b.4.4, in marks."},
 {"u":"38k3.1","q":"How does the undefinable-truth technique yield a true statement PA does not reach, and what does this road give and not give compared with 38i?","a":"Prov(x) picks out the statements PA reaches and no formula picks out the true ones, so the collections differ; every reached statement is true, so some true statement is not reached; this road gives a difference of what can be defined and needs no diagonal for ¬Prov, and it gives no particular statement","w":["It builds U again; this road gives the same statement with a shorter proof","Prov(x) is a truth formula, so every truth is reached; this road gives nothing","The reached statements are more than the true ones; this road gives a false statement that is reached"],"why":"One road ends at a statement, the other at a size."},
 {"u":"38k4.1","q":"Where and how is truth for arithmetic defined, and why does the same argument bar truth for sets from the language of sets?","a":"In the language of sets: N is a set, formulas are sets, and 4d’s recursion builds the truth function as one set; the same diagonal, with codes for formulas of ∈, shows no formula of sets is a truth formula for sets: truth for each language is defined in a richer one and never in itself","w":["In arithmetic, by Prov; sets can define their own truth because they are richer","In the language of sets, by recursion; sets can define their own truth by the same recursion","Nowhere: truth for arithmetic is undefined; so truth for sets is undefined too"],"why":"Every page defined truth for its marks in words, a richer language."},
 {"u":"38k5.1","q":"Why does the undefinable-truth technique not make “true in N” meaningless, and why is Prov(x) no truth formula?","a":"Truth in N is defined, in sets (38a3): it is not defined in arithmetic; and Prov(x) misses U, which is true and not reached","w":["It does make it meaningless; Prov(x) is a truth formula","Truth in N is defined in arithmetic after all; Prov(x) holds of false statements","Truth in N is a matter of opinion; Prov(x) is not a formula"],"why":"Bounded statements even have a search formula for their truth."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38l1.1","q":"What does a set placeholder add to a language, over what does ∀X run in a world, and how do induction and the least upper bound become single statements?","a":"Placeholders X₀, X₁, … with the formulas X(t), “t is in X”, bound by every and some; ∀X runs over every part of the world’s set of members; induction: every part that holds 0 and is closed under next holds everything; the least upper bound: every bounded part that has a member has a least upper bound","w":["New names for sets; ∀X runs over the parts some formula picks out; induction stays one statement for each formula","Placeholders for sets with X(t); ∀X runs over every part of the world; but induction and the least upper bound still need endless lists","Placeholders for functions; ∀X runs over the members; induction becomes a recipe"],"why":"Its accepted name is second-order logic."},
 {"u":"38l2.1","q":"What does the only-the-numbers technique say, and at which three lines, in which world, does its proof use second-order induction?","a":"Any two worlds of PA₂ are matched by a matching that carries 0 and next; in M, to show the pairs h form a function on all of M; in M′, on the outputs, to show h is onto; and in M once more, to show h is one-to-one","w":["Any two worlds of PA are matched; induction is used once, in N","Any two worlds of PA₂ are matched; induction is used three times, all in M′","PA₂ has no world; induction is used to refute it"],"why":"So every world of PA₂ is a copy of N."},
 {"u":"38l3.1","q":"Why is second-order induction false in 38f’s world while every first-order induction is true there, and why does first-order induction reach only some parts of a world?","a":"The part holding the values of the numerals holds 0, is closed under next and is not all of the world, and no formula picks it out; first-order induction speaks only of the parts some formula picks out, countably many in a listed world, while the parts of an endless listed world cannot be listed","w":["Second-order induction is true there too; first-order induction covers every part","The numerals’ part is picked out by a formula, so both inductions fail there; formulas pick out every part","Second-order induction is false there because the world is finite; first-order induction covers no part"],"why":"More parts than members (35c)."},
 {"u":"38l4.1","q":"What does the no-full-calculus technique say, which earlier technique does its proof turn on second-order logic, and why does the finite-parts technique fail there?","a":"No recipe lists exactly the second-order statements true in every world; such a list, kept to the statements PA₂ ⇒ φ, would list the truths of N, against 38i3; and every finite part of PA₂ with all the demands n̄ < c has a world, N with c large, while the whole has none, since every world of PA₂ is N","w":["Some recipe lists them; it uses 38d; the finite-parts technique holds for every logic","No recipe lists them; it turns 35b against them; the finite-parts technique fails because PA₂ is finite","No recipe lists them; it turns 38i3 against them; the finite-parts technique fails because PA₂ has too many worlds"],"why":"One world was bought by handing the hard questions to the theory of sets."},
 {"u":"38l5.1","q":"Why does having only one world not make PA₂ reach every truth of arithmetic, and why is first-order induction for every formula weaker than second-order induction?","a":"No calculus reaches all the second-order truths, so one world does not give proofs; and formulas pick out only countably many parts, while second-order induction covers every part: 38f’s world tells them apart","w":["PA₂ does reach every truth; the two inductions are the same","PA₂ has many worlds; first-order induction is the stronger","PA₂ clashes; neither induction holds in N"],"why":"∀X runs over every part in the set of all subsets."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["38m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["38m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"38m1.1","q":"How does the code-by-primes technique code a string, and why do codes of proofs grow too large to write?","a":"Write the number of each mark as the power of the next prime and multiply: 0 = S0 is 2¹ · 3⁵ · 5² · 7¹ = 85 050; a proof codes a list of lines, each a list of codes, so its code has such numbers as powers","w":["Add the numbers of the marks; proofs are long, so the sums are long","Multiply the numbers of the marks; proofs hold many primes","Write the number of each mark as the power of the next prime and multiply; codes of proofs stay short, since powers shrink"],"why":"Five marks already gave nine digits."},
 {"u":"38m2.1","q":"What does the code-reading technique say, and why does it return the one string with that code?","a":"Divide by 2 as often as it goes, then by 3, then by 5, and on through the primes; the counts are the numbers of the marks in order, and the string ends at the first prime that does not divide; a number has exactly one prime list, and the counts are its powers","w":["Read the digits of the code in pairs; each pair is a mark; digits are unique","Divide by each prime once; the remainders are the marks; remainders are unique","Divide by 2, 3, 5 and on, counting; the counts are the marks; but two strings may share a code, so the first is taken"],"why":"532 445 760 reads back as ¬ S 0 = 0."},
 {"u":"38m3.1","q":"What does the code-test technique say, why is 12 the code of a string and no formula, and why is 10 the code of no string?","a":"A number codes a formula exactly when it is at least 2, its primes are the first k with none skipped, each power is the number of a mark, and the string read back is built as formulas are; 12 = 2² · 3 reads as S 0, a term; 10 = 2 · 5 skips 3","w":["Every number above 1 codes a formula; 12 codes 0 = 0; 10 codes 0 0","A number codes a formula when it is even; 12 is a formula; 10 is odd in its second prime","A number codes a formula when its primes are the first k and the string is a formula; 12 reads as = 0; 10 has too few primes"],"why":"Each test stops, so “x is the code of a formula” is settled by a recipe."},
 {"u":"38m4.1","q":"What does the pair-code technique say, how is a pair read back from its number, and why does that take a square root?","a":"⟨x, y⟩ = (x + y)(x + y + 1)/2 + y, and each number codes exactly one pair; to read z back, find the diagonal d = x + y, the largest d with d(d + 1)/2 ≤ z, then y = z − d(d + 1)/2 and x = d − y; d(d + 1)/2 grows like half a square, so finding d from z undoes a square","w":["⟨x, y⟩ = 2ˣ · 3ʸ; read it back by dividing by 2 and 3; no square root is needed","⟨x, y⟩ = (x + y)(x + y + 1)/2 + y; read it back by halving z; the square root gives y","⟨x, y⟩ = x · y; read it back by factoring; the square root gives x when x = y"],"why":"35a’s listing of pairs by diagonals, counted along."},
 {"u":"38m5.1","q":"Why is the code of 0 = 0 not 1 · 5 · 1, and why is ⟨3, 5⟩ not ⟨5, 3⟩?","a":"The marks’ numbers are powers of the primes, not factors: 2¹ · 3⁵ · 5¹ = 2430; and ⟨3, 5⟩ = 8 · 9/2 + 5 = 41 while ⟨5, 3⟩ = 39: order matters","w":["The code is 1 + 5 + 1 = 7; and both pair codes are 41","The code is 5; and ⟨3, 5⟩ = 39 while ⟨5, 3⟩ = 41","The code is 2430 because 0 = 0 is true; and pairs have no order"],"why":"The last term added is y, not x."});
